---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { locations, model } from '../lib/site';

const hobart = locations.find((l) => l.slug === 'hobart');
---

<Base
  title="How the Aurora Go or No-Go Is Worked Out"
  description="The exact rules behind the verdict: the Kp each place needs, the cloud and darkness cut-offs, how fresh the data is, and where the method breaks."
  path="/how-it-works/"
>
  <h1>How the go or no-go is worked out</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Three checks for each dark hour: is there enough activity for this place, is the sky clear, and is it dark. The
    rules are simple on purpose, and all of them are on this page.
  </p>

  <h2>1. Activity</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      Each place has a magnetic latitude, and from it a Kp level at which the aurora's edge comes within
      {model.easyMarginDeg} degrees. NOAA says the aurora is easily seen at that range. That level is the place's
      "needs" figure: Kp {hobart?.kpVisible} for Hobart, for example. <a href="/kp-needed/">The full table and the working</a>.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Enough:</strong> Kp is at or above what the place needs.</li>
      <li><strong>Borderline:</strong> Kp is one step short.</li>
      <li><strong>Camera range:</strong> lower still, but the edge is within about 1000 km. Counted as a no for the eye.</li>
      <li><strong>Quiet:</strong> anything less.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      For the present hour the Kp is NOAA's live one-minute estimate. For later hours it is NOAA's forecast for that
      three-hour block. Past the three-day forecast, the daily peak from the 27-day outlook stands in, marked "daily".
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>2. Sky</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      Total cloud cover for the place and hour, from MET Norway's global forecast. Up to 30% counts as clear, 31 to 70%
      as partly cloudy, and above 70% as cloudy. Those cut-offs are this site's own choice, not an official standard.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>3. Darkness</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      Only hours when the sun is at least 18 degrees below the horizon are judged. Where the sky never gets that dark,
      as in the far south in midsummer, the hours with the sun 12 degrees down are used and the page says so. The moon
      does not change the verdict, but a moon more than 60% lit that is up for a good part of the night gets a
      warning, because it washes out faint aurora.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Putting them together</h2>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Activity</th><th scope="col">Clear sky</th><th scope="col">Partly cloudy</th><th scope="col">Cloudy</th><th scope="col">No cloud data</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Enough</th>
          <td><span class="pill" data-verdict="go">Go</span></td>
          <td><span class="pill" data-verdict="maybe">Maybe</span></td>
          <td><span class="pill" data-verdict="no">No-go</span></td>
          <td><span class="pill" data-verdict="maybe">Maybe</span></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Borderline</th>
          <td><span class="pill" data-verdict="maybe">Maybe</span></td>
          <td><span class="pill" data-verdict="no">No-go</span></td>
          <td><span class="pill" data-verdict="no">No-go</span></td>
          <td><span class="pill" data-verdict="maybe">Maybe</span></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Camera range or quiet</th>
          <td><span class="pill" data-verdict="no">No-go</span></td>
          <td><span class="pill" data-verdict="no">No-go</span></td>
          <td><span class="pill" data-verdict="no">No-go</span></td>
          <td><span class="pill" data-verdict="no">No-go</span></td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      "Tonight" takes the best hour of the night. After dark, the headline switches to the present hour, with the rest
      of the night underneath.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>What go, maybe and no-go mean</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Go:</strong> the conditions for a display you can see are forecast or present. It is not a promise.</li>
      <li><strong>Maybe:</strong> one thing is short. Worth it if you are keen, close to a dark spot, or carrying a camera.</li>
      <li><strong>No-go:</strong> activity is too low, or cloud is in the way.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <h2>How fresh the data is</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <ul>
      <li>Space weather is fetched from NOAA when you open a page and every 5 minutes while it stays open.</li>
      <li>NOAA updates the live Kp each minute and reissues the 3-day forecast twice a day.</li>
      <li>
        The cloud forecast is kept for 20 minutes in your browser, and on this site's server until MET Norway says a
        newer one may be ready, usually about half an hour.
      </li>
      <li>Darkness and the moon are calculated on your device and need no connection.</li>
      <li>If NOAA's live reading is more than 45 minutes old, the page falls back to the forecast and tells you.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <h2>Where it breaks</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Bursts.</strong> Aurora brightens and fades over minutes. Kp is a three-hour, planet-wide average and
        cannot see that. People sometimes catch a display on a "maybe", and stand under a quiet sky on a "go".
      </li>
      <li><strong>Forecast error.</strong> The Kp forecast is NOAA's best estimate. Storms arrive early, late, or not at all.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Local sky.</strong> The cloud figure is a model value for the area, not a look out of your window. Sea
        fog, low cloud on a ridge and smoke are not in it.
      </li>
      <li><strong>Light and horizon.</strong> Town lights and hills to the south are not measured. Both matter a great deal.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Extreme storms.</strong> Kp stops at 9. The strongest storms push the aurora further than the table
        allows, which is why the far-north pages only ever say "maybe".
      </li>
      <li><strong>Averages.</strong> The Kp each place needs is a rule of thumb for the middle of the night, accurate to about one step.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      For official notices in Australia, use the Bureau of Meteorology's
      <a href="https://www.sws.bom.gov.au/Aurora">Aurora Watch and Aurora Alert</a> service. Every source behind this
      site is listed under <a href="/sources/">Sources and updates</a>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Common questions</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <h3>Kp is high. Why does it say no-go?</h3>
    <p>
      One of three reasons: cloud is forecast over the place, the high Kp falls in daylight hours, or the place is too
      far north for that Kp. The sentence under the verdict says which.
    </p>
    <h3>It said no-go and people still posted photos.</h3>
    <p>
      Cameras gather light for seconds at a time and record aurora the eye cannot see. A photo from a dark beach at
      "camera range" is consistent with a no-go for the eye. The other cause is a short burst that Kp did not catch.
    </p>
    <h3>Why is my town not listed?</h3>
    <p>
      The list covers {locations.length} places to start with. <a href="/my-location/">Check your own coordinates</a> and
      you get the same answer for any spot in Australia or New Zealand.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
